# How to read the split `{{SOURCE_FILENAME}}`

`{{SOURCE_FILENAME}}` was too large to open in a single editor session
(**{{SOURCE_SIZE_MB}} MB / {{SOURCE_LINE_COUNT}} lines**). It has been split into
**{{CHUNK_COUNT}}** byte-exact, LF-aligned chunks in this folder. This file tells
humans *and* machines how to navigate them.

> Drop this `INSTRUCTIONS.md` next to the chunks. Replace the `{{PLACEHOLDERS}}`
> when emitting from the `large-text-file-stream-split` skill.

---

## TL;DR

- Chunks live in [`./`](./) (this folder).
- Each chunk is **~{{CHUNK_SIZE_MB}} MB** — opens instantly in any editor.
- Concatenating all chunks in name order reproduces `{{SOURCE_FILENAME}}`
  **byte-for-byte**.
- Use [`INDEX.csv`](./INDEX.csv) to map a global line number to its chunk.

---

## File naming convention

```text
{{BASE_NAME}}_part_NNN_of_{{CHUNK_COUNT}}__lines_SSSSSSS-EEEEEEE.txt
              │                              │       │
              │                              │       └─ end   global line in this chunk (inclusive)
              │                              └─ start global line in this chunk (inclusive)
              └─ this chunk's serial (zero-padded, 1-based, out of {{CHUNK_COUNT}} total)
```

Example: `{{BASE_NAME}}_part_042_of_{{CHUNK_COUNT}}__lines_0780123-0800450.txt` is
chunk **42** and contains the **global lines 780,123 → 800,450** of
`{{SOURCE_FILENAME}}`.

Because every name carries `NNN_of_{{CHUNK_COUNT}}`, the predecessor and successor
are trivial. Alphabetical sort = correct chunk order (the count is zero-padded).

