## How these chunks were produced

Per the `large-text-file-stream-split` skill in the `ai-agents` repo
(`.agents/skills/large-text-file-stream-split/`):

```powershell
.\scripts\build_split_log.ps1
.\scripts\split_log.exe '<path-to-original>\{{SOURCE_FILENAME}}' .\<chunk-dir> {{CHUNK_COUNT}} {{BASE_NAME}}
```

Algorithm summary:

- Streams the source in a 4 MiB buffer (constant memory).
- Per-part target = `remaining_bytes / remaining_parts`, recomputed each part
  (self-corrects for LF-boundary slack so the split lands on exactly N parts).
- Cuts only on `\n`, so each chunk is a valid prefix of complete lines and
  concatenation reproduces the source byte-for-byte.

---

## Rules for humans and agents

- **Never edit a chunk in place.** They are derived artifacts. Edit the source (or,
  more realistically, regenerate it) and re-run the splitter.
- **Do not commit the chunks to Git.** Add the chunk folder and the source to
  `.gitignore`.
- **Always use `INDEX.csv` for line/byte ↔ chunk mapping.** Do not infer positions
  from filenames alone — line ranges are not uniform (chunks are byte-balanced,
  not line-balanced).
- **When grepping, sort your hits by chunk name** to read them in source order.

